<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Óscar Romero</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Saint Óscar Romero</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Óscar Romero was Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> from 1977 to 1980. Initially considered a conservative, he became an outspoken critic of military repression and death squads, using his radio sermons to denounce killings. He was shot dead while celebrating mass on 24 March 1980, a murder that the UN Truth Commission attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roberto-daubuisson">Roberto D'Aubuisson</a>. He was canonised in 2018.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oscar-romero.jpg" alt="Óscar Romero" width="320" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arzobispado de San Salvador; Congregatio de Causis Sanctorum · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monse%C3%B1or_Romero_(colour).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Óscar Arnulfo Romero y Galdámez was born on 15 August 1917 in Ciudad Barrios, San Miguel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. He attended the local school, which offered only three grades, and was then tutored privately. His father trained him in carpentry, but Romero raised the idea of studying for the priesthood and entered the minor seminary in San Miguel at the age of thirteen. He completed his studies at the Gregorian University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, took a Licentiate in Theology in 1941 and was ordained in Rome on 4 April 1942.</p><p>In 1943 his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> summoned him home. On the way he and a friend, Father Valladares, were detained by the Cuban police, probably because they had come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a>, and spent months in internment camps before release from a hospital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Romero was appointed auxiliary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> on 25 April 1970 and Bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de María, a poor rural region, on 15 October 1974. On 3 February 1977 he became Archbishop of San Salvador. The government welcomed the appointment, while many priests were disappointed, especially those who supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberation-theology">liberation theology</a> and feared that his conservative reputation would hurt its commitment to the poor. After the murder of his friend and fellow priest Rutilio Grande, Romero became an outspoken critic of the military government.</p><p>He broadcast his weekly sermons on the church's radio station YSAX, listing disappearances, tortures and murders each Sunday. By listener surveys 73 per cent of the rural population and 47 per cent of the urban listened regularly. His diocesan weekly Orientación carried lists of cases of torture and repression. His relationship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberation-theology">liberation theology</a> was debated: his biographer Jesús Delgado holds that he supported the Catholic vision of it rather than a materialist one.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 23 March 1980 Romero called on Salvadoran soldiers, as Christians, to stop carrying out the government's repression. The next evening he was shot while celebrating Mass at the chapel of the Hospital de la Divina Providencia. His funeral on 30 March was attended by more than 250,000 mourners. No one has been convicted of the killing, but the United Nations Truth Commission for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> concluded that Major Roberto D'Aubuisson, a death squad leader, had ordered it.</p><p>In 2010 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a> proclaimed 24 March the International Day for the Right to the Truth Concerning Gross Human Rights Violations and for the Dignity of Victims, in recognition of his role. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-francis">Pope Francis</a> declared him a martyr on 3 February 2015, beatified him on 23 May 2015 and canonised him on 14 October 2018. A statue of him stands above the Great West Door of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>, among ten twentieth-century martyrs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Óscar Romero” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The murder of Archbishop <b>Óscar Romero</b> while saying mass on 24 March 1980 made him a worldwide symbol of liberation theology and of the defence of the poor; he was canonised by Pope Francis in 2018.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In El Salvador, figures such as Archbishop <b>Óscar Romero</b> became symbols of resistance against military regimes and human rights abuses.</blockquote><a href="/movement/liberation-theology">Liberation theology · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ciudad Barrios, in what is now <a href="/countries/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.77,-88.27&amp;year=1957" aria-label="Ciudad Barrios on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Óscar Romero"><image href="/maps/globes/g27000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5144 3834h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5144 3834h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.77,-88.27&amp;year=1957">What was here in 1957? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
